WEBVTT

00:08.058 --> 00:11.110
Most AI coding tools can generate a first draft.

00:11.483 --> 00:20.096
The hard part is the second week: remembering the decisions, noticing when a change breaks one, and letting you correct the direction without losing the history.

00:20.736 --> 00:23.092
Muse Recall is built for that second week.

00:23.348 --> 00:24.705
This is a live run.

00:24.904 --> 00:33.019
I start a fresh project and save one constraint: a warm sunset palette, with a soft coral accent and dark text, no neon.

00:33.565 --> 00:35.538
Spark writes a small memory game.

00:35.713 --> 00:39.079
I read the source, check the preview, and approve v1.

00:39.452 --> 00:43.340
The decision is stored with its project, its source and its history.

00:43.538 --> 00:46.091
Now a new session, with no request history.

00:46.266 --> 00:48.239
I ask only for a Next Level button.

00:48.449 --> 00:51.466
The staged source still uses the sunset accent.

00:51.839 --> 00:55.507
Guard has no correction to compare, so it reports not verified.

00:55.879 --> 00:56.842
Nothing ships.

00:56.913 --> 00:58.200
I reject the change.

00:58.387 --> 00:59.547
I change my mind.

00:59.920 --> 01:06.188
In Memory, I correct the decision to a cool ocean palette, with a clear blue accent and deep navy text.

01:06.561 --> 01:10.263
The sunset record stays auditable but becomes superseded.

01:10.706 --> 01:13.351
The ocean decision is now the only active one.

01:13.562 --> 01:15.418
I ask for the same button again.

01:15.675 --> 01:18.042
The model keeps the old sunset colors.

01:18.682 --> 01:23.998
This time Guard has the correction to compare, and it flags the reintroduced sunset palette.

01:24.545 --> 01:29.733
That is the point of an advisory check: it shows the evidence, but it does not block me.

01:30.106 --> 01:31.510
I reject it myself.

01:31.697 --> 01:35.039
Then I ask for the project's current art direction from memory.

01:35.354 --> 01:38.546
The source flips to ocean, and the sunset color is gone.

01:39.093 --> 01:42.679
Guard reports no violation detected by its supported checks.

01:43.121 --> 01:44.444
I approve v2.

01:44.619 --> 01:49.505
v1 stays recoverable in version history, and rollback follows the recorded parent.

01:49.681 --> 01:58.027
The contribution is the integration: memory you can correct, a change you can review, and evidence that memory changes generated code.

01:58.400 --> 02:08.244
In the pre-registered Phase 17 trial, a remembered rule cut original-rule violations from thirty-seven of fifty-seven to zero of fifty-eight on Muse Spark,

02:08.384 --> 02:12.203
and forty-nine of sixty to one of sixty on Muse Spark Open.

02:12.576 --> 02:15.001
Retyping the rule by hand matched it.

02:15.443 --> 02:23.325
Phase 17's correction only released restrictions; the sunset-to-ocean replacement is Phase 12A, one run per condition.

02:23.698 --> 02:26.495
Six fixed tasks, not general correctness.

02:26.937 --> 02:28.817
Persistent memory is not new.

02:28.958 --> 02:32.300
Making it correctable, reviewable and measurable is the point.
